     1	# Thesis library
     2	
     3	> Status: foundation frozen, October 20, 2026. D149 adds extraction-owned grouping; D165 puts it in the default pipeline. D169: the matching step runs on Jev by default (`poc-v23`); D168 states the Replace row's full definition and the succession answer it needs. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163). Only recorded decisions apply; open questions and two pull-mode proposals remain separate.
     4	> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Thesis library → Details. This PRD also owns Matching labels. Recorded decisions take precedence. Historical discussion: `docs/views-rebuild-log.md`.
     5	
     6	## Purpose
     7	
     8	A thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare evidence within its thesis to repeat, replace or add. Extra source references alone do not trigger rejudgement. Extraction determines group associations; the writer preserves them after resolving final thesis IDs under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction).
     9	
    10	## Rationale
    11	
    12	- Clear structures connect theses, ladders and evidence to source items.
    13	- A thesis is a question with a state, not an assertion. Opposing views move the same state's direction rather than creating opposite duplicate theses.
    14	- A fixed, verifiable ladder states in advance what would disprove the view, analogous to explicit analyst scenarios or predefined investment exit conditions. Do not change it after observing the outcome.
    15	- The model judges from all evidence still in force and dependency conclusions, at low temperature, rather than adjusting its previous score. Replacement follows statement date, so late old statements do not restore obsolete evidence.
    16	- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.
    17	- A thesis contains one period and one due date, generally 6–12 months; intermediate quarterly reports are evidence. A thesis on a binding obligation a universe company takes on (a guarantee, financing, an equity stake, a purchase commitment) may set its own check day up to 18 months after the item, because the signing can be checked long before what it finances is built (D185).
    18	- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.
    19	- Evidence needs the same search-then-compare discipline. Otherwise February's 12% capex guidance and April's 25% revision remain side by side, forcing every judgement to rediscover which is current.
    20	- Record repeat/replacement decisions once. Keep replaced evidence in history. A retelling alone should not require another model judgement to establish that nothing changed.
    21	- Use two rungs for yes/no instead of forcing five.
    22	- The model chooses dependencies like an analyst; code rejects cycles. Apparently mutual dependencies indicate poorly defined questions.
    23	- Rejudge when inputs change. A changed conclusion includes a new rung or materially changed supporting facts/numbers. A repeated Fed statement should not trigger hundreds of dependants; AMZN capex moving from 12% to 25% can trigger NVDA even within the same rung.
    24	- Start matching with a general-purpose model, retain records, obtain human labels and later train a faster smaller model.
    25	
    26	## Structure
    27	
    28	<!-- generated: structure -->
    29	```
    30	Thesis library
    31	├─ Subject → Companies, cryptocurrencies, commodities and themes in the universe
    32	│   └─ Thesis → A question + a ladder + a due date (Immutable once written. Five rungs, or two for yes/no. When matching opens a thesis, only one may be open for the same subject and focus at a time.)
    33	│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model judges from facts. A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)
    34	│       ├─ Link → Evidence (Store evidence once with sources and tier. Its push belongs to the link. Replaced evidence remains in history and no longer counts.)
    35	│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)
    36	│       └─ Succession → The previous period's thesis (Successive periods of the same focus form a chain)
    37	├─ Research group → Organise theses by a continuing business process (Associate during extraction; full rules are in “Research groups: associate during extraction”)
    38	└─ Append-only records → Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labelling records (Append at every step, without editing or deleting. Read records up to a historical date to recover its judgement.)
    39	```
    40	<!-- /generated: structure -->
    41	
    42	## Two thesis kinds
    43	
    44	<!-- generated: kinds -->
    45	| Kind | Subject kind | Ladder rungs | Example |
    46	|---|---|---|---|
    47	| Asset thesis | Tradable subjects: companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities | Five rungs: strongly bullish, bullish, neutral, bearish, strongly bearish. Two for yes/no: bullish and bearish. These describe the business impact; set them at creation and keep them fixed. | How will NVDA FY2027 China revenue compare with FY2026? |
    48	| Theme thesis | Macro, policy and industry themes | Ordered outcomes from one end to the other: five rungs, or two for yes/no. Name outcomes without judging them good or bad. Dependent asset theses specify which outcome benefits them. | How will the Fed's target-rate upper bound after its last 2026 meeting compare with now? (Large cut … large hike) |
    49	
    50	The same event can affect assets differently: rate cuts can be bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM net interest income. Theme theses therefore do not label outcomes good or bad.
    51	<!-- /generated: kinds -->
    52	
    53	## Ladder
    54	
    55	<!-- generated: ladder -->
    56	```
    57	Two kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)
    58	├─ Asset thesis: numeric → NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 | Five rungs describing NVDA's business impact (Due on the FY2027 annual earnings date)
    59	│   ├─ Strongly bullish → Up at least 50%
    60	│   ├─ Bullish → Up at least 10% but under 50%
    61	│   ├─ Neutral → Up or down by less than 10%
    62	│   ├─ Bearish → Down at least 10% but under 80%
    63	│   └─ Strongly bearish → Down at least 80% (At most one fifth remains)
    64	└─ Theme thesis: event → China export licensing for chips covered by the April 2025 rule, including H20 and MI308, as of April 15, 2026 | Five ordered outcomes, without good/bad labels (Use the rules in force on that date)
    65	    ├─ Rung 1 → Full ban; licence applications no longer accepted
    66	    ├─ Rung 2 → Licences required; none issued
    67	    ├─ Rung 3 → Licences required and issued; some conditional
    68	    ├─ Rung 4 → Licences required and issued; all unconditional
    69	    └─ Rung 5 → Licences no longer required
    70	```
    71	
    72	1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed.
    73	2. Each rung is verifiable: a numeric interval or event stage. Rungs must be exhaustive and non-overlapping, so exactly one applies at expiry. For example: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Every boundary belongs to one rung. “Up a lot/a little” cannot be checked. Avoid vague partial outcomes; specify the partial state, such as conditional licences, as a distinct rung. State the comparison underlying neutral.
    74	3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.
    75	4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.
    76	5. Numeric theses name a year. Non-company quantities use calendar years: the publication year is this year and the following year is next year, each ending December 31. Company quantities use the fiscal year containing publication day and its successor. Never use the computer clock. For a given quantity, open at most one thesis for each of this year and next year; do not open later years. The due date is the quantity's publication date.
    77	6. Set the due date when the debate can be resolved, usually 6–12 months away. Intermediate quarterly reports are evidence, not separate theses.
    78	<!-- /generated: ladder -->
    79	
    80	## State
    81	
    82	<!-- generated: positions -->
    83	|  | Meaning | Decision maker | Example |
    84	|---|---|---|---|
    85	| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges at low temperature from facts. Reconsider all evidence still in force, including each item's replaced predecessor to show the change, plus current dependency states and reasons and, for an asset thesis, the theme block. Do not increment or decrement the previous score. Cite evidence and compare with the conclusion before this round to state whether it changed. Do not rejudge if only source references were added and dependency conclusions did not change. | After the 8-K: strongly bearish |
    86	
    87	- Every judgement states its basis in a fixed response shape: evidence IDs such as e12 and e15, plus dependency thesis IDs such as #1 when their changes triggered rejudgement.
    88	- Code checks cited IDs against records: evidence must belong to this thesis and not be replaced; cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then human review after repeated failure. This checks references, not whether the judgement is correct, like verifying a cited page exists.
    89	- Each judgement also declares whether the conclusion changed and explains why, comparing with the state and reasons before this round, including during retries. That earlier conclusion is for comparison, not incremental scoring. A rung change or a material change in the supporting facts/numbers counts: AMZN capex guidance moving from 12% to 25% growth changes the conclusion even within one rung. Repetition does not. A changed conclusion triggers dependent theses in the same round. Code requires changed=true for a first judgement or rung change; otherwise refuse under the same correction rules.
    90	- For historical judgement, read the records saved on that date. Do not ask the model again; a new sample can differ.
    91	<!-- /generated: positions -->
    92	
    93	## Four evidence tiers
    94	
    95	<!-- generated: tiers -->
    96	| Tier | Example | Role |
    97	|---|---|---|
    98	| Fact | Official actions, published figures and completed transactions | Can establish which rung holds and move the state |
    99	| Party statement | Company guidance, management statements and official speeches | Can move the state only |
   100	| Third-party view | Sell-side analysts, media analysis and experts | Can move the state only |
   101	| Rumour | Unnamed-source claims and unattributed statements | Can move the state only |
   102	
   103	- The model determines the size of the push and gives reasons. No tier has a fixed score.
   104	- Count independent original sources. Twenty outlets retelling one exclusive are twenty references to one evidence item, not twenty independent confirmations.
   105	- Tier follows who confirmed it. An unnamed-source report that Commerce is issuing licences is a rumour. An official or company confirmation is factual evidence replacing it. The rumour remains a rumour in history.
   106	- Store evidence once without an intrinsic direction. Each rejudgement writes its push on the evidence–thesis link. One statement can affect multiple theses differently.
   107	<!-- /generated: tiers -->
   108	
   109	## Dependencies: reading the arrows
   110	
   111	<!-- generated: dependencies -->
   112	```
   113	B depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)
   114	├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)
   115	│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons
   116	│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)
   117	│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)
   118	└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)
   119	    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons
   120	    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)
   121	    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”
   122	```
   123	
   124	- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.
   125	- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.
   126	- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushes it; no edge is written for it. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.
